[@ "Return of the mysterious stranger"]

*Ding-dong!* ding-donged the doorbell. Francesca answered. "Oh, another detective?" she asked the marvelously-breeched man before her. "Are you here to bring back Manuel?"
"Actually, I'm a piano tuner," said the Beach Detective, perfectly pretending not to be surprised.
"Oh, another one?" said Francesca. "Well, you'd best come in."
The beach detective followed Francesca wordlessly into the kitchen/dining-room.
"Miss Prudence and I are just having tea in the house, now best friends,"[@ J wrote this verbiage as a proposal, and I adapted it verbatim. ~M] explained Francesca.
This was so. But Beach's interest was immediately drawn to a another personage in the room, who was just hovering[$ the type of hovering where your feet are firmly on the floor] near the table, not sitting at it. This man, though apparently idling impotently, had such a commanding presence that Beach recognized him immediately as "Marvinlust Maoi" from the bus, although he was now completely cleanshaven and his face was caked with white greasepaint like a mime's.[$He was also, for some reason, wearing a cowboy hat.]
"Marvinlust?" asked Beach, more out of surprise than anything.
"Oh, no, no," said the man. "I'm not Marvinlust Maoi. I'm... Cicisbeo Servente. [@ see, eg, https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cicisbeo / https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cicisbeo] Yes."
"OK."
Prudence's mind leapt back to her mission briefing from her handlers: "get hired at that weird pseudo-secret coffee shop Reginald maintains and see if you can figure out what the heck is going on"[@ C wrote this verbiage as a proposal, and I included it it verbatim. ~M]. Somehow, she felt like she was getting farther and farther away from that second goal.
"Story- and fortune- teller extraordinairé!" claimed Cicisbeo. [@  I guess this accent mark doesn't make any sense. Even extraordinair̀e would make more sense. ~M]
"Alright," said Beach.
"I'm here to keep ’Chesca company after the tragiqué[@ yeah again this doesn't really make any sense. He just talks like that I guess. ~M] and mysterious disappearance of her husband Ol' Whatsit Guy and the arresting arrest of her arresting handmanden Manuel "the manual". And here to read the palm of Prudence. To observe the lines of prudence."[@ In my headcanon as I write this 1. Cicisbeo actually knows who Reginald is and is fond of him and is just having fun. 2. Prudence's fate is to date Thomas. 3. Maybe Cicisbeo will seductively hold her hand or something. ~M]
Beach eyed the hat and coat on the floor with some suspicion.

[C; 500 words]

*It's like someone dissolved or teleported or became incorporeal or otherwise ceased applying force keeping the hat and coat aloft and filled out while simultaneously[$ **Whil**e **s**imul**t**aneously, or **Whilst** for short. This is e**n**tirely, c**o**mpletely, and **t**otally true.] applying those forces necessary to fold them,* he thought, although not in so many words[$ Strictly speaking, taking them off and folding them and setting them neatly on the floor fits this description; the Beach Detective is doing a real bang-up job so far.].

"May I take your coat?" asked Francesca. She found the presence of Cicisbeo indefinably cheering, which was helping her re{t/g}ain her customary equanimity.

"Alright." Would she fold it up on the floor? No, the Beach Detective's fine overcoat was hung in an ordinary fashion (at least in as ordinary a fashion as a woman like Francesca could undertake any task)[@  ~J] on an ordinary hook, next to Prudence's apron.

Prudence was wishing she had her vape within reach, not languishing in her apron's pocket. Not due to an addiction, but because she would have liked something to do with her hands other than the obvious. The room—indeed the whole house—was of that extreme and understated opulance which does not draw attention with gaudy materials and flashy arrangements, but instead positively sheds attention like dew off a duck's back with the Right materials, the Correct arrangements. Prudence nevertheless attended to it. Her nascent observational abilities were burgeoning. With some shyness, she extended her hand to the palmist.

"I hesitate to sound in any way ungrateful," Francesca challenged gently, "but I don't know of a scheduled service call for our piano today. Or even how you could know we have a piano."

"Oh, I have an amazing ability to intuit things," lied the Beach Detective with easy grace. "It just felt like there was a piano in need of a good sprucing up here on this island."

"I see."

"So you know your way around p.-forte, eh, my good sir, my good sir[@ The conclusion of the sentence: "Can ye no' go a wee bitty faster, my good man, my good man?" from _A Hat Full of Sky_ by Terry Pratchett has often struck me, so I'm copying it a bit here. ~C]?" Cicisbeo's tone, hitherto jovial, became for a moment serious. "How are you at fish[@ A reference to the old joke set-up: "What's the difference between a piano and a fish?" ~C]?" He was having a grand time. He took Prudence's proffered appendage. He held it communicatively, conveying his full awareness of the warm animality of the blood pulsing beneath the skin, and the complexity of the intellect communicated via the nerves, and the purposive agency of the personhood which both, in part, comprised. It conveyed with remarkable fluency[@ I think there's a better word than this, but I can't think of it right now. Intricacy? Articulation? ~C] that he was vividly aware that he was a man and she a woman, a very attractive woman whom he would ordinarily seek the highest expression of love with, but for the circumstances[$ not just the circumstances of their currently being in company. Additional circumstances.].

Prudence looked out the window, having just happened to catch the short, trenchcoated gaze[@ She caught his gaze... before looking out the window? ~J][@ I think originally I had written, "Prudence was looking out the window". And then edited it to be less passive to the detriment of the understandability of the action. I may edit this later, but for now I'm leaving it. ~C] of Hugo Wright[$ You called it folks, it's everyone's favorite character Hugo Wright, Wright Heare's canonical identical twin half-cousin, introduced and solely mentioned in a footnote in _16 unkind play_].

[@ M's intention had been that Cicisbeo's cover was also being a piano tuner, which was why Francesca said, "Oh, another one?" to the Beach Detective. I didn't realize this, so in my section Francesca makes no mention of Cicisbeo being a piano tuner, when it seems that maybe she would. However, what she does say is consistent with Cicisbeo also being an unexpected piano tuner, so it still mostly works. Possibly F already gently challenged C in the same manner and was satisfied with his answer, or perhaps she was less suspicious of him. And both of them pretending to be piano tuners promises to be comedy gold, so we should keep it if possible. ~C]
[@ I request that we not kill off Hugo Wright as quickly as the others but if them's the breaks, that's okay. it would also be pretty funny  ~C]
[@ also, it feels significant that the Martin Eeny dossier is back in the Digsxby home, presumably in the coat that Francesca just took off the Beach Detective ~C]

[J; 438 words]

She didn’t know that it was Hugo Wright, of course. She was thinking it was more likely Wleft Theare or Wmiddle Yone, having heard neither of their deaths nor of their identical twin half-cousins.
“Prudence,” said Cicisbeo, crouching, reading, “Your palm shows Love and Fate intersecting most significantly.” He placed his finger at the intersection of two creases, and when she turned from the window to look down at her palm, she found her nose being flicked by that same finger in a sort of juvenile micro-prank.
“This is no laughing matter,” Cicisbeo scolded her, even though she was too surprised to laugh. She frowned at him. Cicisbeo laughed lightly and stood up.
“Well, how’s about that piano?” he asked.
Prudence glanced out the window again, but saw no sign of Hugo Wright or any of his ilk.

---

Sonic had spent her Thursday morning swimming in Lake Taupō, walking through the woods, eating yoghurt, reading in a hammock, and trying to relax. She was on vacation.
Now, in the late morning, she reclined in the hammock by the stream and stared blankly at the natural beauty about her. Having had her fill of words and things and having set aside her book, her mind skittered about restively[@ not to be confused with restfully ~M] over the smooth terrain of no distractions. Various business and personal matters had troubled her for the first week or so of her vacation, but now she had moved on to the Big Questions. What’s it all about? What are we doing here? What am I specifically doing here? What happens when we die? Of course, those smug bastards on the flying islands go to heaven, but what about everyone else?[@ What a twist! Maybe they go to secret double heaven. ~M] After all, Sonic often found herself off the islands in her interminable and just[@ not to be confused with merely ~M] international busying-about, and might so perish. She was technically on an island right now, but she couldn’t remember if people on non-flying islands went to heaven or just those on flying islands. Maybe it was just about being airborne? She briefly considered taking a leap of faith and ending it all (before she hit the ground) to find out, but it was, like all her recent thoughts, an idle one.
*Br-r-ring!*[@ This is supposed to be the Sonic ring pickup sound effect :)] Drat! There went her stupid smartwatch. Her assistant was the only one who could reach her here, so it must be something inane and not worth the disruption. She made a mental note to get a new assistant and glanced at the message on her watch face despite herself.
“I just smoked two banana backwoods in Blockbuster parking lot no one has ever done it!!!”[^@backwoods] read the message.
Ok.

[^@backwoods]: While I was trying to figure out what the message should be, I received this text from a friend, totally non sequitur, and thought it fit perfectly and would be funny to include verbatim, so that’s what I did.

[M; 442 words]

